  "body": "This is discussed briefly at https://github.com/whatwg/streams#other (scroll down a bit).\n\nTransform streams (\"meat grinder\" in @dominictarr's terminology) certainly make sense as a single object. It's clear that you do `write()` on one end and `read()` resulting data.\n\nBut what about duplex streams (\"telephone\" in @dominictarr's terminology)?\n\n`{ in, out }` seems clearer in some cases, e.g. [serial ports](http://whatwg.github.io/serial/#serialport-interface) or TCP sockets. But in other cases, e.g. web workers, it seems less desirable. Mainly because it's convenient the treat the web worker \"as if\" it were a transform stream, as in the\n\n``` js\nhttp.get(\"http://example.com/video.mp4\")\n    .pipe(videoProcessingWebWorker)\n    .pipe(document.query(\"video\"));\n```\n\nexample.\n\n---\n\nAnother possible dimension: we could make `pipe` auto-detect `{ in, out }` pairs, so that you could pipe through `{ in, out }` style streams. (It would write to the `in` and return the `out`.) To be clear, any object literal in the appropriate form would work; no brand checking.\n",
